{
  "$comment": "Message search operator grammar v1: portable conformance cases for clients that parse the same query syntax. `now`, `since` and `until` are local wall-clock times (no zone): a client evaluates them in its own timezone, so day boundaries follow local midnight, including across DST changes. `since` and `until` are inclusive unix-second bounds; null means unbounded. `from` and `in` are the raw operator values; resolving them (me, @name, #channel, @person) is client-side. `fullText` says the text needs full-text (web search) syntax: a closed \"phrase\" or a -word exclusion. `positive` says it has a word or phrase to search for. Unknown operators, unrecognized date values (including years below 100) and anything inside quotes (an unclosed quote runs to the end) stay in `text`; every `\"` opens or closes a phrase, even inside a word, as in Postgres websearch_to_tsquery; inside quotes, `-` and `or` are words; outside, `or` is an operator only between two terms, and a lone `-` excludes the next word or phrase. Expected outputs are literal, not generated by the code under test.",
